(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Ionic equilibrium is the balance between ions and un-ionised molecules in a solution. The equilibrium rules from Class 11 are applied here to acids, bases, salts, buffers and sparingly soluble salts.","w":["Equilibrium is dynamic: the forward and reverse changes go on at the same rate.","A catalyst does not change the position of equilibrium."],"x":["Many reactions take place in water, and many substances in water split into ions. Ionic equilibrium is the balance between the ions and the molecules that have not split, in such a solution. The same rules of chemical equilibrium that you learned in Class 11 are used here.","In this chapter you will use them for acids and bases, the pH of a solution, the reaction of salts with water, buffer solutions, and salts that dissolve only a very little in water. These ideas are used in the lab, in the body (the pH of blood), in medicine and in industry.","Study tip: the numericals in this chapter all follow a few steps. Write the equilibrium, write the expression for the constant, put in the concentrations, and solve. Practise the logarithms, because most answers are pH values."],"e":"Acetic acid in water: CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺. Only a small part of the molecules splits, so most of the acid is still present as molecules at equilibrium."},"3.2":{"s":"Electrolytes give ions in water; non-electrolytes, such as sugar, stay as molecules. Strong electrolytes (NaCl, HCl, NaOH) ionise almost completely. Weak electrolytes (acetic acid, ammonium hydroxide) ionise only a little. The degree of dissociation α is the fraction that has ionised.","w":["Strong does not mean concentrated. A strong electrolyte ionises almost completely even when dilute.","A weak electrolyte has a small degree of ionisation that rises with dilution."],"x":["A substance that gives ions in water, so that the solution carries a current, is an electrolyte. A non-electrolyte, such as sugar or urea, dissolves as molecules, and the solution does not carry a current.","Strong electrolytes are almost fully ionised in water. Examples: NaCl, HCl, NaOH, KNO₃. Weak electrolytes are ionised only a little, and most of the substance is present as molecules. Examples: acetic acid, ammonium hydroxide, carbonic acid.","The degree of dissociation, α, is the fraction of the dissolved molecules that have split into ions. For a strong electrolyte α is close to 1. For a weak electrolyte α is much less than 1. A weak electrolyte is in equilibrium with its ions, so the rules of equilibrium can be used for it.","Common mistake: thinking that a strong electrolyte is the same as a concentrated solution. Strength tells how much it ionises, and concentration tells how much is dissolved."],"e":"A 0.1 M solution of HCl has [H⁺] = 0.1 M, since it ionises fully. A 0.1 M solution of acetic acid has [H⁺] of only about 0.001 M, since α is about 1 percent."},"3.3":{"s":"Three ways to define acids and bases. Arrhenius: acids give H⁺ in water and bases give OH⁻. Brønsted–Lowry: an acid donates a proton and a base accepts one; each acid has a conjugate base. Lewis: an acid accepts an electron pair and a base donates one.","w":["Water is amphoteric: an acid towards NH₃ and a base towards HCl.","Every Brønsted base is a Lewis base, but not every Lewis acid is a Brønsted acid (BF₃ has no proton)."],"x":["There are three ways to define acids and bases. In the Arrhenius theory, an acid gives H⁺ in water and a base gives OH⁻. It works only for water solutions. In the Brønsted–Lowry theory, an acid is a proton donor and a base is a proton acceptor. In the Lewis theory, an acid accepts an electron pair and a base gives an electron pair.","In the Brønsted–Lowry theory, acids and bases come in conjugate pairs. When an acid gives up a proton, what is left is its conjugate base: HCl gives Cl⁻. When a base accepts a proton, it becomes its conjugate acid: NH₃ gives NH₄⁺. A strong acid has a weak conjugate base, and the reverse is also true.","Water is amphoteric: it acts as an acid with a base like NH₃, and as a base with an acid like HCl. The Lewis theory is the widest. BF₃ and AlCl₃ are Lewis acids because they have an empty orbital, though they have no proton. Every Brønsted base is a Lewis base, but not every Lewis acid is a Brønsted acid.","Common mistake: writing the conjugate base with the wrong charge. Remove H⁺ from the acid, and the charge goes down by one."],"e":"In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, NH₃ is a base and its conjugate acid is NH₄⁺. H₂O is an acid and its conjugate base is OH⁻."},"3.4":{"s":"Strong acids and bases ionise fully. A weak acid has a dissociation constant Ka; a larger Ka means a stronger acid. Ostwald's dilution law: for a weak electrolyte α = √(K/c), so it ionises more as the solution is diluted.","f":[{"x":"K_a = [H^(+)]*[A^(-)]/[HA]","n":"Dissociation constant of a weak acid HA; K_b for a weak base is written the same way with OH^(-).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.49"},{"x":"K_a = alpha^2*c/(1 - alpha)","n":"Ostwald's dilution law; c = concentration in mol/L.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.50"},{"x":"alpha = sqrt(K_a/c)","n":"Weak acid with alpha much less than 1; for a weak base alpha = sqrt(K_b/c).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.50"},{"x":"[H_3O^(+)] = alpha*c","n":"Hydronium ion concentration of a weak monobasic acid.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.51"}],"w":["Diluting a weak acid raises α but lowers [H⁺], because c falls faster than α rises."],"x":["A strong acid or base ionises fully. A weak acid HA is in equilibrium: HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, with the dissociation constant K_a = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]. A larger K_a means a stronger acid. A weak base has K_b in the same way, with OH⁻.","Suppose the concentration is c and the degree of dissociation is α. At equilibrium [H⁺] = [A⁻] = αc and [HA] = c(1 − α). So K_a = α²c/(1 − α). For a weak acid, α is small, so 1 − α is nearly 1, and K_a = α²c. This is Ostwald's dilution law. It gives α = √(K_a/c), and [H⁺] = αc = √(K_a c).","How to use it: to find the pH of a weak acid, find [H⁺] = √(K_a c), then pH = −log[H⁺]. Check afterwards that α is small, say less than 5 percent. If it is not, solve the quadratic equation.","On dilution, α increases, because c is smaller. But [H⁺] decreases, because c falls faster than α rises. Common mistake: thinking that a more dilute weak acid has more H⁺."],"e":"Find the pH of 0.10 M acetic acid, K_a = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵. [H⁺] = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.10) = 1.34 × 10⁻³ M. pH = 2.87. α = 1.34 × 10⁻³/0.10 = 1.3 percent, so the approximation is fine."},"3.5":{"s":"Water ionises very slightly into H⁺ and OH⁻. Their product, Kw, is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C. pH = −log[H⁺] and pOH = −log[OH⁻], and pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C. pH 7 is neutral, below 7 is acidic, and above 7 is basic.","f":[{"x":"K_w = [H_3O^(+)]*[OH^(-)] = 1.0*10^-14","n":"Ionic product of water at 298 K.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.5, p.51"}],"w":["The ionic product of water rises with temperature, so neutral water has pH below 7 when hot; it is still neutral."],"x":["Pure water ionises a very little: H₂O + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻. The product of the two ion concentrations is a constant, the ionic product of water: K_w = [H₃O⁺][OH⁻]. At 298 K, K_w = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴. In pure water, the two concentrations are equal, 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol/L each.","In an acid solution, [H₃O⁺] is greater than 10⁻⁷ and [OH⁻] is smaller. In a basic solution it is the other way round. But their product is always K_w at that temperature. If you know one concentration, find the other from [OH⁻] = K_w/[H₃O⁺].","K_w rises with temperature, because the ionisation of water takes in heat. At 373 K, it is about 5 × 10⁻¹³, so the pH of pure hot water is about 6.1. It is still neutral, because [H₃O⁺] equals [OH⁻].","Common mistake: thinking that a solution is neutral only at pH 7. It is neutral when the two ion concentrations are equal. pH 7 is the neutral value at 298 K only."],"e":"A solution has [H₃O⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M. Then [OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴/10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ M. The solution is acidic, since [H₃O⁺] is greater than [OH⁻]."},"3.6":{"s":"pH tells how acidic a solution is: it is the negative logarithm, to base 10, of the hydronium ion concentration in mol/dm³. pOH is the same quantity for hydroxide ions. In water at 298 K the two add up to 14. A solution with pH 7 is neutral, below 7 acidic, above 7 basic. Each step of one pH unit is a tenfold change in the hydronium ion concentration.","f":[{"x":"pH = -log_10([H_3O^(+)])","n":"Concentration in mol/dm^3. Neutral water at 298 K has [H_3O^(+)] = 1.0 × 10^-7, so pH = 7.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"},{"x":"pOH = -log_10([OH^(-)])","n":"The same for hydroxide ions.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"},{"x":"pH + pOH = 14","n":"Valid for aqueous solutions at 298 K (K_w = 1.0 × 10^-14).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"}],"w":["pH 3 has ten times more hydronium ions than pH 4.","pH + pOH = 14 holds only at 298 K; at other temperatures the ionic product of water differs."],"x":["Concentrations of H₃O⁺ like 0.0000001 are awkward to write. So we use the pH scale: pH = −log₁₀[H₃O⁺], with the concentration in mol/dm³. In the same way, pOH = −log₁₀[OH⁻]. At 298 K, pH + pOH = 14, since K_w = 10⁻¹⁴.","A solution with pH 7 is neutral, pH below 7 is acidic, and pH above 7 is basic. The scale is logarithmic. One unit of pH is a factor of 10 in the concentration, so pH 3 has 10 times as many H₃O⁺ ions as pH 4, and 100 times as many as pH 5.","How to find the pH: for a strong acid, [H⁺] equals the concentration of the acid. For a strong base, find [OH⁻] first, then pOH, and then pH = 14 − pOH. If two solutions are mixed, find the total moles and divide by the total volume. Never average the pH values.","Common mistake: using pH = log[H⁺], without the minus sign, or using pH = 14 − pOH at a temperature other than 298 K."],"e":"Find the pH of 0.01 M NaOH. [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M, so pOH = 2 and pH = 14 − 2 = 12. For 0.01 M HCl, pH = 2."},"3.7":{"s":"Hydrolysis: the ions of some salts react with water and change the pH. A salt of a strong acid and strong base (NaCl) gives a neutral solution. Weak acid with strong base (CH₃COONa) gives a basic one. Strong acid with weak base (NH₄Cl) gives an acidic one. If both are weak, the pH depends on their Ka and Kb.","w":["Strong acid + strong base salt: neutral. Strong acid + weak base: acidic. Weak acid + strong base: basic.","For a weak acid + weak base salt compare Ka and Kb: Ka > Kb acidic, Ka < Kb basic, equal neutral (CH₃COONH₄)."],"x":["Hydrolysis is the reaction of the ions of a salt with water, which changes the pH of the solution. It depends on the strength of the acid and the base from which the salt is made.","A salt of a strong acid and a strong base, like NaCl, does not hydrolyse, and the solution is neutral. A salt of a weak acid and a strong base, like CH₃COONa, gives a basic solution: CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. A salt of a strong acid and a weak base, like NH₄Cl, gives an acidic solution: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺.","If both the acid and the base are weak, both ions hydrolyse, and the pH depends on K_a and K_b. If K_a is greater than K_b, the solution is acidic. If K_a is less than K_b, it is basic. If they are about equal, as for CH₃COONH₄, the solution is nearly neutral.","For a salt of a weak base, the hydrolysis constant is K_h = K_w/K_b, and [H⁺] = √(K_h c). Common mistake: using the ion of the strong part. Look at the ion that comes from the weak acid or the weak base. That is the one that reacts with water."],"e":"Find the pH of 0.10 M NH₄Cl, K_b = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵. K_h = 10⁻¹⁴/1.8 × 10⁻⁵ = 5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰. [H⁺] = √(5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 0.10) = 7.5 × 10⁻⁶ M, so pH = 5.13."},"3.8":{"s":"A buffer resists big changes in pH when a little acid, base or water is added. An acidic buffer is a weak acid plus its salt (acetic acid with sodium acetate); a basic buffer is a weak base plus its salt. Henderson's equation: pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]). Blood is a buffered solution.","f":[{"x":"pH = pK_a + log_10([salt]/[acid])","n":"Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer; pK_a = -log_10(K_a).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.8, p.56"},{"x":"pOH = pK_b + log_10([salt]/[base])","n":"Basic buffer; then pH = 14 - pOH.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.8, p.56"}],"w":["Dilution does not change a buffer's pH: the ratio [salt]/[acid] stays the same."],"x":["A buffer solution resists a large change in pH when a little acid or base is added, or when it is diluted. An acidic buffer is a weak acid and its salt with a strong base, like CH₃COOH with CH₃COONa. A basic buffer is a weak base and its salt with a strong acid, like NH₄OH with NH₄Cl.","How it works, for an acidic buffer: the acid takes up any OH⁻ that is added, and the acetate ion takes up any H⁺ that is added. So [H⁺] hardly changes. The pH is found by the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation: pH = pK_a + log([salt]/[acid]). For a basic buffer, pOH = pK_b + log([salt]/[base]).","If the salt and the acid are in equal amounts, pH = pK_a. A buffer works best when the ratio is between 0.1 and 10, so the pH is within 1 of the pK_a. When a little strong acid or base is added, change the moles of the acid and the salt first, and then use the equation.","Dilution does not change the ratio, so the pH stays the same, but the capacity is lower. Common mistake: using concentrations of the acid and the salt in the wrong order in the log. The salt is on top. Blood is buffered by H₂CO₃ and HCO₃⁻."],"e":"A buffer has 0.1 mol of CH₃COOH and 0.1 mol of CH₃COONa in 1 L, pK_a = 4.74, so pH = 4.74. After adding 0.01 mol NaOH, the acid is 0.09 and the salt is 0.11. pH = 4.74 + log(0.11/0.09) = 4.83."},"3.9":{"s":"Take a saturated solution of a sparingly soluble salt. Multiply the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient. The result is constant: the solubility product Ksp. It links to solubility S; for AgCl, Ksp = S². A precipitate forms when the ionic product exceeds Ksp.","f":[{"x":"K_sp = [B^(y+)]^x*[A^(x-)]^y","n":"Solubility product of B_xA_y from saturated-solution concentrations.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.9, p.58"},{"x":"K_sp = x^x*y^y*S^(x + y)","n":"In terms of molar solubility S: AgBr S^2, PbI_2 4S^3, Al(OH)_3 27S^4.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.9, p.58"}],"w":["Precipitation happens only when the ionic product is GREATER than Ksp.","After mixing two solutions, halve (or rescale) each concentration before finding the ionic product."],"x":["Some salts, like AgCl and PbI₂, dissolve only a very little in water. In a saturated solution, the solid is in equilibrium with its ions: AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻. The product of the ion concentrations, each raised to the power of its coefficient, is a constant at a given temperature. It is the solubility product, K_sp.","For a salt B_xA_y, K_sp = [B^y+]^x [A^x−]^y. If the molar solubility is S, then K_sp = x^x y^y S^(x+y). So AgCl gives S², PbI₂ gives 4S³ and Al(OH)₃ gives 27S⁴. Use these to go from S to K_sp and back.","To decide if a precipitate forms, find the ionic product Q with the actual concentrations. If Q is greater than K_sp, a precipitate forms. If Q is equal to K_sp, the solution is saturated. If Q is less than K_sp, no precipitate forms.","When two solutions are mixed, the volume increases, so find the new concentrations first. Common mistake: using the concentrations before mixing, and forgetting to raise to the power of the coefficient."],"e":"K_sp of PbI₂ is 1.4 × 10⁻⁸. S = (K_sp/4)^(1/3) = (3.5 × 10⁻⁹)^(1/3) = 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol/L. [I⁻] = 2S = 3.0 × 10⁻³ M, and [Pb²⁺] = 1.5 × 10⁻³ M."},"3.10":{"s":"Adding an ion that is already in the equilibrium pushes it back and suppresses ionisation: the common ion effect. Adding NH₄Cl suppresses the ionisation of NH₄OH. It also lowers the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt. Salt analysis uses it to precipitate groups of cations separately.","w":["A common ion lowers the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt, but Ksp itself stays the same."],"x":["If an ion is added to a solution in which it is already present in an equilibrium, the equilibrium shifts back. This is the common ion effect (Le Chatelier's principle). It makes the ionisation of a weak acid or a weak base smaller, and it makes a sparingly soluble salt less soluble.","Example: add NH₄Cl to NH₄OH. The extra NH₄⁺ pushes NH₄OH ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ to the left, so [OH⁻] falls. Add sodium acetate to acetic acid, and [H⁺] falls in the same way.","For solubility: in AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻, added Cl⁻ from NaCl makes the equilibrium go to the left, so less AgCl dissolves. K_sp does not change, because it depends only on the temperature. If [Cl⁻] is 0.1 M, the solubility is S = K_sp/0.1.","Salt analysis uses this idea: in group II of the analysis, the S²⁻ concentration is kept low with H⁺, so that only some sulfides precipitate. Common mistake: saying that K_sp falls when a common ion is added. Only the solubility falls."],"e":"K_sp of AgCl = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. Solubility in water = √(1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 1.3 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L. Solubility in 0.1 M NaCl = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰/0.1 = 1.8 × 10⁻⁹ mol/L, which is much smaller."}};
